nted."], "part_used": "Fruit (seed); also fresh leaf (cilantro)", "researched": "2026-09-15", "rule": "Temperature: stored value clear (a definite Cool, anchored in the record to the Ayurvedic cooling potency), so it stays Cool. Moisture: stored value clear (a definite Neutral), so it stays Neutral.", "rule_general": "FINAL founder rules 2026-09-15: clear stored modern value stays; unclear or missing value set by majority of first-hand pre-1900 sources; tie broken by the oldest first-hand written source, Neutral if the oldest sources are themselves tied; copied readings count once; group statements, second-hand reports, other parts and other species shown but not counted; snigdha and 潤 = Moist, rūkṣa and 燥 = Dry.", "sources": [{"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool); differs from the app moisture (source dry, app neutral).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Seeds are hot in the first degree. Linseed, Fenugreek, Coriander … Dry … In the third. Dill, Smallages, Bishop's Weed, Annis, Caraway, Cummin, Coriander", "gloss": "Coriander seed is hot in the first degree and dry in the third.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, 'Seeds' (College list, Culpeper's degree table); also 'Seeds or Grains': 'Coriander seed, hot and dry'. Gutenberg #49513 lines ~17658 and ~19196-19227", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool).", "assigns_moisture": null, "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true