 "Warm", "corpus": "chinese", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "甘松香 … 根氣味甘温無毒 {好古曰平}", "gloss": "Root sweet, warm, not toxic (Wang Haogu says neutral).", "locator": "卷十四 草之三, 甘松香", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "Identity: 甘松香 is Nardostachys (Chinese material often N. chinensis, treated by many as the same species as N. jatamansi). Porter Smith 1871 equates Kan-sung-hiang with Nardostachys Jatamansi.", "short": "Bencao Gangmu (1596)", "source": "李時珍, 本草綱目 (Li Shizhen, Bencao Gangmu), Siku Quanshu text", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Chinese", "url": "https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/本草綱目_(四庫全書本)/", "year": "1596"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool).", "assigns_moisture": null, "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "chinese", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "甘松香 … 辛甘溫。芳香理諸氣。", "gloss": "Pungent, sweet, warm.", "locator": "卷二 草部, 甘松香", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "Same identity note as Bencao Gangmu.", "short": "Bencao Congxin (1757)", "source": "吳儀洛, 本草從新 (Wu Yiluo, Bencao Congxin)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Chinese", "url": "https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/本草從新", "year": "1757"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool); agrees with the app moisture (dry).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Of Spikenard, Indian, and Cheltic. … They are both hot and dry.", "gloss": "Indian and Celtic spikenard are both hot and dry.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, Roots, 'Nardi Spicae, Indicae, Celticae' (Gutenberg #49513 line ~15676)", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "Names Indian spikenard (this plant) and Celtic spikenard together ('both'). Counted as naming this species; see the questions.", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. The whole passage follows Galen's account of nard and names him ('according to Galen'), so under the final rules it is a second-hand report and is not counted.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "The true Spikenard or Indian Nard hath a heating and drying faculty, being (according to Galen) hot in the first degree [yet the Greek copy hath the third] and dry in the second.", "gloss": "Indian nard has a heating and drying faculty; the degrees are Galen's.", "locator": "Book II, Chap. 441 'Of Mountain Setwall, or Nardus', The Temperature and Virtues B. Scan OCR line 73857 (near p. 1079).", "not_counted_why": "The whole passage follows Galen's account of nard and names him ('according to Galen'), so under the final rules it is a second-hand report and is not counted.", "note": "The whole passage follows Galen's account of nard and names him ('according to Galen'), so under the final rules it is a second-hand report and is not counted.", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics transcription and checked against the 1636 printing scanned at archive.org)", "tier": "second-hand-report", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt", "year": "1633"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. A second-hand report: the text credits another writer.", "assigns_moisture": null, "assigns_temperature": "Cool", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature