nt once; group statements, second-hand reports, other parts and other species shown but not counted; snigdha and 潤 = Moist, rūkṣa and 燥 = Dry.", "sources": [{"agreement": "Counted: agrees with the app temperature (warm); differs from the app moisture (source moist, app dry).", "assigns_moisture": "Moist", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "बालं बिल्वफलं ग्राहि दीपनं पाचनं कटु । कषायोष्णं लघु स्निग्धं तिक्तं वातकफापहम् ॥", "gloss": "bālaṃ bilvaphalaṃ grāhi … kaṣāyoṣṇaṃ laghu snigdhaṃ: the young bael fruit is binding, kindles and aids digestion, pungent, astringent and HOT, light, UNCTUOUS, bitter, removes vata and kapha.", "locator": "Āmrādiphala varga 7.57", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Bhavaprakasha (16th c.)", "source": "Bhāvamiśra, Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu (Sanskrit original)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Ayurvedic (Indian)", "url": "https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/भावप्रकाशः/पूर्वखण्डे_भावप्रकाशनिघण्टुः", "year": "16th century"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app moisture (source moist, app dry).", "assigns_moisture": "Moist", "assigns_temperature": null, "corpus": "ayurvedic", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "phalaṃ tu komalaṃ snigdhaṃ guru saṃgrāhi dīpanam", "gloss": "The tender (unripe) fruit is UNCTUOUS, heavy, binding, kindles digestion.", "locator": "Rājanighaṇṭu 11.191", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Rajanighantu (c. 15th c.)", "source": "Narahari, Rājanighaṇṭu (Sanskrit original)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Ayurvedic (Indian)", "url": "http://gretil.sub.uni-goettingen.de/gretil/corpustei/transformations/plaintext/sa_narahari-rAjanighaNTu.txt", "year": "c. 15th century"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. Part not stated; the verse opens the run of the five 'great root' trees, so it is most likely the root. Not counted for the unripe fruit.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "श्रीफलस्तुवरस्तिक्तो ग्राही रूक्षोऽग्निपित्तकृत् । वातश्लेष्महरो बल्यो लघुरुष्णश्च पाचनः ॥", "gloss": "śrīphalas tuvaras tikto grāhī rūkṣo … laghur uṣṇaś ca: bilva is astringent, bitter, binding, DRY, raises fire and bile, light and HOT.", "locator": "Guḍūcyādi varga 4.13", "not_counted_why": "Part not stated; the verse opens the run of the five 'great root' trees, so it is most likely the root. Not counted for the unripe fruit.", "note": "Part not stated; the verse opens the run of the five 'great root' trees, so it is most likely the root. Not counted for the unripe fruit.", "short": "Bhavaprakasha (16th c.)", "source": "Bhāvamiśra, Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu (Sanskrit original)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Ayurvedic (Indian)", "url": "https://sa.wikisource.org/wiki/भावप्रकाशः/पूर्वखण्डे_भावप्रकाशनिघण्टुः", "year": "16th century"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. A second-hand report: the text credits another writer.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry (half-ripe)", "assigns_temperature": "Cool (unripe)", "corpus": "ayurvedic", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "they consider the ripe fruit to be hot and dry, the very young fruit cold in the second degree, and the half ripe fruit cold in the first and dry in the second degree", "gloss": "Reports Muslim (Unani) physicians after Muhammad bin Zakariya: ripe hot and dry; very young cold (2nd); half-ripe cold (1st) and dry (2nd).", "locator": "Vol. I pp. 277-278, AEGLE MARMELOS, History, Uses &c.", "not_counted_why": "A second-hand report: the text credits another writer.", "note": "", "short": "Pharmacographia Indica I (1890)", "source": "Dymock, Warden & Hooper, Pharmacographia Indica, Vol. I", "tier": "second-hand-report", "tradition": "Ayurvedic (Indian)", "url": "https://archive.org/download/b21960343_0001/b21960343_0001_djvu.txt", "year": "1890"}], "sources_agree": false, "stored_before": {"moisture": "Dry", "temperature": "Warm (Ayurvedic virya: ushna). The unripe fruit is heating and drying, kindling digestion and clearing cold damp; this distinguishes it from the sweet ripe fruit, which is treated as cooling (sheeta)."}, "stored_value_status": {"moisture": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Dry"}, "temperature": {"status": "clear", "why": "a definite Warm per the Ayurvedic ushna virya for the unripe fruit"}}}$j$::jsonb);

